# 5. Running Commands Inside Container
When a Docker container is running, we often need to interact with it to check logs, troubleshoot issues, view files, install packages, or monitor running applications. Docker provides the docker exec command to execute commands inside a running container without stopping or restarting it.


# 1. docker exec
 docker exec runs a command inside an already running container.
> Why is it used?
Check files
Install packages
Debug applications
View logs
Execute Linux commands
Access the container terminal

Syntax
// docker exec [OPTIONS] CONTAINER COMMAND
Example
// docker exec mycontainer ls
This lists all files inside the mycontainer container.

# 2. Interactive Mode (-it)
Interactive mode allows users to directly access and interact with the container's terminal.

> Options Used
-i (Interactive): Keeps standard input open.
-t (Terminal): Allocates a terminal session.
Example
// docker exec -it mycontainer bash
This command opens an interactive Bash shell inside the container.

> Benefits
Execute Linux commands directly.
Troubleshoot applications.
Modify files temporarily.
Verify installed software.

# 3. Bash Inside a Container
Most Linux-based Docker containers provide a shell environment such as Bash.
> Accessing Bash
// docker exec -it mycontainer bash
After running the command, you enter the container shell:
// root@container-id:/#
Now you can run Linux commands such as:
pwd
ls
cd
cat
ps

> If Bash is Not Available
Some lightweight images (like Alpine Linux) do not include Bash.
> Use:
docker exec -it mycontainer sh


# 4. Running Processes Inside a Container
A container runs one main process, but additional commands and processes can be executed inside it.
> View Running Processes
// docker exec mycontainer ps
Displays currently running processes.

Monitor Processes
// docker exec -it mycontainer top
Shows real-time CPU and memory usage.
> Run a Specific Command
// docker exec mycontainer date
Displays the current date and time inside the container.